The '''Iskhal-Badalga North Line''' (Burawi: ''Dhaqso Iskhal-Badalga''), formerly known as the '''Burawi Main Line''', is a high-speed rail line in [[Burawa]] connecting the [[South Hudur]] and [[Haro (Metropolitan Administration)|Haro]] metropolitan areas. The line runs from [[Iskhal International Airport]] in [[Dhartoonga]] to [[Badalga Central Station]] in [[Badalga]], a distance of approximately 306 kilometers. It was the first high-speed rail line constructed in Burawa, opening in 1972 after six years of construction.

The Iskhal-Badalga North Line is the busiest railway line in Burawa, carrying approximately 95 million passengers annually as of 2026. The line operates six service patterns: two express services (A and B), two limited-stop services (C and D), and two all-stop local services (E and F). The line was built over and along the original [[Iskhal-Badalga Line]], a conventional railway completed in 1832.

In 2011, the parallel [[Iskhal-Badalga South Line]] opened to relieve congestion on the North Line, providing an alternative route on the southern side of the [[Buran River]]. However, induced demand following the South Line's opening resulted in continued growth in total corridor ridership rather than a reduction in North Line usage.

== Route ==
{{See also|Iskhal-Badalga South Line}}

The Iskhal-Badalga North Line connects the [[South Hudur]] and [[Haro (Metropolitan Administration)|Haro]] metropolitan areas. The line begins at [[Iskhal International Airport]] in [[Dhartoonga]], runs south through the Iskhal metropolitan area, then northeast to [[Badalga]], running north of the [[Buran River]]. The line serves 11 stations over a distance of 306.13 kilometers.

