== History ==

=== Iskhal-Badalga Line (1832–1966) ===

{{main|Iskhal-Badalga Line}}

The original [[Iskhal-Badalga Line]] was completed in 1832 as part of the early expansion of the Burawi railway network. The conventional railway connected the port city of [[Iskhal]] with the industrial center of [[Badalga]], serving as a critical freight and passenger corridor for over a century. By the mid-20th century, however, the line had become congested, and travel times were considered inadequate for modern needs.

=== Planning and construction (1966–1972) ===

In 1966, the [[BuRail]] corporation initiated planning for a high-speed rail line along the Iskhal-Badalga corridor. The project aimed to reduce travel times between the two metropolitan areas and demonstrate Burawa's industrial capabilities. Construction began later that year, with the line built largely over and alongside the existing conventional railway.

The project faced significant engineering challenges, including the construction of elevated viaducts through urban areas and tunnels through coastal hills. The first segment, from [[Iskhal Main Station]] to [[Badalga Central Station]], opened in 1972 at a cost of approximately 2.3 billion hanti (equivalent to approximately 12 billion hanti in 2026 values).

=== Extensions (1974–2003) ===

The success of the initial line prompted extensions to serve additional demand. In 1974, the line was extended 17.63 kilometers south from Iskhal Main to [[Iskhal Central Station]], providing better access to the central business district.

In 2003, a further 35.26-kilometer extension opened to the newly constructed [[Iskhal International Airport]] in [[Dhartoonga]], providing direct rail access to the airport from both the Iskhal metropolitan area and Badalga.

=== Capacity constraints and the South Line (2000–2011) ===

By 2000, the Iskhal-Badalga North Line was operating significantly above its designed capacity. Ridership had reached approximately 110 million passengers annually, leading to chronic overcrowding and delays. The [[BuRail]] corporation and the Burawi government determined that a parallel line was necessary to relieve congestion.

Construction of the [[Iskhal-Badalga South Line]] began in 2000. The new line, running on the southern side of the Buran River, opened in 2011. Following the South Line's opening, total corridor ridership continued to grow due to induced demand, with the North Line maintaining approximately 95 million annual passengers and the South Line carrying approximately 75 million.

== Rolling stock ==

{{main|Dhaqso}}

The Iskhal-Badalga North Line is operated by [[Dhaqso]] series trainsets manufactured by the [[Burawa Train Materials Manufacturing Corporation]] (BTMMC). The line has seen five generations of rolling stock since its opening.

=== Dhaqso-I (1972–1990) ===

The original Dhaqso-I trainsets entered service with the line's opening in 1972. These trains had a maximum operating speed of 250 km/h and consisted of 12-car formations. The Dhaqso-I fleet was retired in 1990.

=== Dhaqso-II (1985–2005) ===

The Dhaqso-II generation introduced improved reliability and a maximum speed of 280 km/h. These trainsets operated alongside the earlier Dhaqso-I fleet and continued in service until 2005.

=== Dhaqso-III (1998–2018) ===

The Dhaqso-III generation featured modernized interiors and a maximum speed of 300 km/h. These trains were the first to feature the current two-class seating arrangement (Commuter and Economy).

=== Dhaqso-IV (2010–present) ===

The current Dhaqso-IV fleet operates exclusively on the Iskhal-Badalga North Line, with a maximum speed of 320 km/h. Standard formations consist of 12 cars with 960 seats, while extended peak-hour formations consist of 16 cars with 1,280 seats.

=== Dhaqso-V (2022–present) ===

The latest Dhaqso-V generation operates primarily on the [[Iskhal-Badalga South Line]], with a maximum speed of 350 km/h. A small number of Dhaqso-V trainsets operate on the Iskhal-Badalga North Line during peak hours.

== Ridership ==

The Iskhal-Badalga North Line is the busiest railway line in Burawa and one of the busiest high-speed rail lines in the world. Annual ridership has grown from approximately 15 million in 1972 to over 95 million in 2026.

