The correlation between the South Line's opening and sustained high ridership on the North Line is attributed to induced demand: improved capacity and frequency attracted new passengers who had previously avoided rail travel due to congestion.

== Stations ==

=== Iskhal International Airport ===

{{main|Iskhal International Airport}}

[[Iskhal International Airport]] station serves as the southern terminus of the Iskhal-Badalga North Line, though it is located north of [[Iskhal Main Station]] due to the line's routing. The station is integrated into the airport terminal, providing direct rail access for air passengers. The station opened in 2003 as part of the airport's construction.

=== Iskhal Central ===

[[Iskhal Central Station]] is a historic station in the southern part of [[Iskhal]], serving the older districts of the city. The station was extensively remodeled in the late 20th century to accommodate high-speed rail services. The station provides connections to the [[Iskhal-Badalga South Line]], [[Kankadadka-Iskhal Line]], [[Kaysoor-Iskhal Line]], [[Iskhal-Qallahere Line]], and [[Iskhal-Adado Line]]. The station features 42 platforms and serves as the origin for Express B, Limited D, and Local F services.

=== Iskhal Main ===

[[Iskhal Main Station]] is the primary station serving the central business district of [[Iskhal]]. All services on the Iskhal-Badalga North Line stop at this station, making it the busiest station on the line outside of the termini. The modern station was constructed as part of the original high-speed line opening in 1972. The station provides connections to the [[Iskhal-Badalga South Line]], [[Kankadadka-Iskhal Line]], [[Kaysoor-Iskhal Line]], and [[Iskhal-Qallahere Line]].

=== Badalga Central ===

{{main|Badalga Central Station}}

[[Badalga Central Station]] serves as the northern terminus of the Iskhal-Badalga North Line. The station is integrated with the [[Haro Metro]] system and provides connections to the [[Iskhal-Badalga South Line]], [[Badalga-Laasqoray Line]], [[Badalga-Maianka Line]], and [[Badalga-Khar Line]]. The original station was constructed in 1832 with the conventional railway, with the high-speed platforms added in 1972.

